1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,910
A drone can be ready while the job is not.

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:05,850
A crew arrives, but site access changed
overnight.

3
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:08,530
Yesterday's approval still looks complete.

4
00:00:08,810 --> 00:00:12,450
GroundProof, built by Shivam Gupta, connects
each inspection job

5
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:16,390
to the evidence behind it and flags the jobs
that need a fresh human decision.

6
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:27,710
Here are six fictional inspection jobs
across three sites.

7
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:31,130
Open one job to see the access notice,
checklist,

8
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:33,310
and insurance evidence it depends on.

9
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:37,430
GroundProof records exactly what was
reviewed, when it was captured,

10
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:39,450
and which version the person approved.

11
00:00:40,210 --> 00:00:42,950
Each sign-off has a specific set of
supporting evidence.

12
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:49,470
Now, a simulated site closure changes one
notice.

13
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:52,590
Exactly three jobs lose their previous
approval.

14
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:56,710
Their combined planned job value is $4,800
in this example.

15
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:00,770
That is the work affected by the change, not
a savings claim.

16
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:03,570
The other three jobs keep their own evidence
state.

17
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:11,910
An old sign-off cannot be reused.

18
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:15,190
The coordinator sees the earlier notice
beside the current one,

19
00:01:16,070 --> 00:01:18,370
explains the decision, and keeps the jobs on
hold.

20
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:23,410
Missing, expired, blocked, or unavailable
evidence also prevents sign-off.

21
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:25,770
These checks follow explicit rules.

22
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:29,390
A language model does not decide whether an
aircraft may fly.

23
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:37,870
Next, restore the notice and accept the
current evidence.

24
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:41,850
Notice what does not happen. The old mission
approval stays revoked.

25
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:45,970
The coordinator must separately review and
sign off this job again.

26
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:49,990
The audit records each step.
Operator-supplied permission records

27
00:01:49,990 --> 00:01:53,710
can also have an expiry date, checked
against the planned job time.

28
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:07,910
The decision packet preserves the exact
evidence behind that fresh sign-off.

29
00:02:08,630 --> 00:02:12,050
It includes an integrity check that can be
verified outside the app.

30
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:15,910
That checks the recorded content, not
whether a document is genuine.

31
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:18,390
Now, a source fails.

32
00:02:19,150 --> 00:02:20,650
GroundProof keeps the last

33
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:24,110
snapshot visible for investigation and holds
the dependent work.

34
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:38,590
The Proof Lab runs 15 scenarios through the
same evidence rules used by the application.

35
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:44,290
Here are the actual results. Now we capture
an official Boston page through Anakin.

36
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:48,690
The source text, provider, time, and hash
are recorded.

37
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:53,110
This is a real web capture, separate from
the fictional site closure drill.

38
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,250
Our first target customer is a drone
inspection

39
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:10,470
operator coordinating repeat work across
multiple sites.

40
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:13,710
We propose $199 per month,

41
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:17,710
then test that price against actual review
time and useful alerts.

42
00:03:18,590 --> 00:03:21,450
We seek one operator for a four-week shadow
pilot.

43
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:25,310
GroundProof keeps internal approvals tied to
current evidence.

44
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:28,090
Flight decisions stay with the remote pilot.
